C# 15 (in preview with .NET 11) lets break and continue target an outer loop by label:<p>outer: for (int x = 0; x &lt; xMax; x++)
{<p><pre><code>   for (int y = 0; y &lt; yMax; y++)
   {

       if (ShouldSkipRest(x, y))
          continue outer;

       if (ShouldExitAll(x, y))
          break outer;
   }</code></pre>
}<p>Honestly, &quot;goto&quot; never even comes to mind when I write an algorithm. I find it hard to follow, so when I need to leave nested loops I extract a method and &quot;return&quot; instead.<p>Do you use &quot;goto&quot; in real code? Do you think you&#x27;ll use labeled break&#x2F;continue?<p>https:&#x2F;&#x2F;learn.microsoft.com&#x2F;en-us&#x2F;dotnet&#x2F;csharp&#x2F;whats-new&#x2F;csharp-15#labeled-break-and-continue
